e traffic</article-title><source><italic>Nature</italic></source><year>2005</year><volume>438</volume><issue>7068</issue><fpage>597</fpage><lpage>604</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16319879</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="fig1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>					<italic>d</italic><italic>N</italic>/<italic>d</italic><italic>S</italic> ratio distribution of orthologous genesequences from <italic>C. parvum</italic> and <italic>C. hominis</italic>. Black bars represent thefrequency of gene sequences in each <italic>d</italic><italic>N</italic>/<italic>d</italic><italic>S</italic> category. For <italic>d</italic><italic>N</italic>/<italic>d</italic><italic>S</italic> &#x0003c; 1, categorysize is 0.05; for <italic>d</italic><italic>N</italic>/<italic>d</italic><italic>S</italic> &#x0003e; 1, category size is 0.5.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CFG2008-879023.001"/></fig><fig id="fig2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>					<italic>d</italic><italic>N</italic>/<italic>d</italic><italic>S</italic> ratio distribution of orthologous genes with orwithout functional annotation. Black bars represent gene with unknown function,while gray bars indicate known function.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CFG2008-879023.002"/></fig><fig id="fig3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>					<italic>d</italic><italic>N</italic>/<italic>d</italic><italic>S</italic> ratio distribution of orthologous genesequences with or without TM/SP structural domains. Black bars represent geneswith TM/SP structural domain while gray bar represents the gene without TM/SP. TM:transmemebrane domain; SP: signal peptide.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="CFG2008-879023.003"/></fig><table-wrap id="tab1" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Summary of RT-PCR results.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" colspan="2" rowspan="